A||||14||Dick McHaffie||My check engine light went on||||8||
Z||000000||Dick McHaffie||02-18-2000||08:27 PM||||My Daughter came home this morning after driving our 85 Fiero GT.<P>She said it started idling really fast and then the check engine light came on.  The light went out when she got back out on the highway, but it was on when she left it running in the Driveway.<P>The only thing I have done to it recently is change the Dist. cap and Rotor.  I drove it about 90 miles and it ran like a top yesterday and the day before.<P>Any Ideas?||||reg||8
Z||000001||fiero56||02-18-2000||09:29 PM||||If the light came on, there should be at least one stored code in the ECM. To retrieve it/them do the following:<BR>1. take off the black plastic cover surrounding the cigarette lighter.(t15 torx)<BR>2. There are too rows of connectors in the ALCL. (with ignition off) Insert a paper clip bent in half into the two connectors on the bottom row passenger side. Now turn the ignition to "off". The light should blink a code 12 three times(1 flash, pause, 2 flashes=12). After blinking the 12 three times, any stored codes will blink. These are what you need to fix the problem.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrFeeArrow||02-18-2000||10:30 PM||||This seems to be a common reacurrence. I've had this problem for many years and changed out components related to code 35 "IAC" with mixed results. I just live with it now.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||02-18-2000||10:31 PM||||I think you mean to turn ignition to "run" but do not start the engine. All error codes are 2 digits so it will blink once pause then blink 2 times. This is code 12. It is a normal indication. After it flashes 12 three times any codes stored will start. Each error will flash 3 times until all codes are done then it will flash 12 again.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||02-18-2000||10:37 PM||||You are correct Oreif,<BR>guess I was typing ahead of my thinking. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTBro||02-19-2000||09:35 PM||||Before he gets the codes, my first guess would be the O2 sensor.  <P>Let's see if I am right.||||reg||4
Z||000006||fiero56||02-19-2000||09:50 PM||||O2 sensor is possilble, but I have another 6 or so ideas flying around here.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Lance||02-20-2000||12:36 PM||||is there a place to figure out what the codes meen?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||02-20-2000||01:09 PM||||Go to Autozone.  You can get a book and diagnostic key (replaces the paper clip) for free.  The book explains most of the codes, how to check for them, and also, how to check some of the sensors (EGR, etc.).||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||02-20-2000||01:54 PM||||I'm in the process of typing up a page for my site about the codes. If you have and need to find out what they mean, just post them here. Someone will reply.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Dan||02-20-2000||03:06 PM||||Check this out. <A HREF="http://www.syty.org/d&e.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.syty.org/d&e.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Dick McHaffie||02-23-2000||08:38 PM||||Thanks a bajillion folks, you are a great help.<P>I finally got around to doing the paperclip, here is what I got.<P>24 (Vehicle speed Circuit)<BR>32 (exhaust recirc. circuit)<BR>33 (Manifold absolulte pressure low vac)<BR>35 Idle speed Error<BR>44 02 sensor lean exhaust.<P>We had the problem last Friday, haven't had a problem since, and its been driven about 160miles.<P>Which codes does it flash first, newest or oldest?<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000012||Tom88gt||02-23-2000||10:03 PM||||Are you sure about the 32 code?  My Haynes manual says thats the EGR vaccum solenoid.  I've been looking for one but haven't found it locally yet.  Supposedly Auto Zone sells it for $30, but not in my town.  Not even in their data base.||||reg||5
Z||000013||theogre||02-24-2000||07:56 PM||||check the EGR hardware and plumbing. sounds like a big vac leak.||||reg||1
Z||000014||DRH||02-25-2000||11:18 AM||||Dick,<P>The codes are flashed smallest to biggest, nothing to do with the order or how many times they were set.<P>That is a lot of codes to be set at one time. Especially since it got better all on it's own. My first guess is you have some loose wiring somewhere making the ECM "think" all that stuff went wrong. You could also have some old codes confusing the issue. Codes are stored until all power to the ECM is interrupted for a few seconds. I would reset it by disconnecting the battery ground for a few seconds. Then if it happens again you will know they are all new codes.<P>David.<BR>||||reg||1
